-\subsection{Stacking motifs and polytypism}
-
-The structural unit treated here by the disorder model is the I--Pb--I trilayer. Ordered 2H-like and laterally shifted 6H-like motifs differ primarily in the basal translation accumulated from one trilayer to the next. This makes PbI$_2$ a natural case for a Hendricks--Teller-type rod treatment in which the ordered layer form factor is retained and the layer-to-layer correlation enters as an analytical correction along $L$.\cite{HendricksTeller1942}
-
-
-\begin{figure}[H]
-  \centering
-  \begin{minipage}[t]{0.3\linewidth}
-    \centering
-    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/results_pbi2/2H.png}
-    \vspace{0.5ex}
-    \textbf{(a)}
-  \end{minipage}
-  \begin{minipage}[t]{0.3\linewidth}
-    \centering
-    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/results_pbi2/6H_R-3.png}
-    \vspace{0.5ex}
-    \textbf{(b)}
-  \end{minipage}
-  \caption{Polytypes of the PbI$_2$ disorder model. Letters next to trilayers represent the laterally shifted phase. (a) 2H PbI$_2$ has trilayers stacked such that the Pb is aligned. (b) 6H PbI$_2$ has trilayers stacked with Pb shifted laterally by a phase of $\frac{\pi}{3}.}
-  \label{fig:pbi2_ewald_cylinder_comparison}
-\end{figure}
-
-For a basal translation $(\Delta x,\Delta y)$ in fractional hexagonal coordinates, a reflection $(h,k,L)$ acquires the phase
-\begin{equation}
-  \exp\!\left[i2\pi(h\Delta x+k\Delta y)\right].
-  \label{eq:pbi2_translation_phase}
-\end{equation}
-The slip convention used here corresponds to the basal translation
-\begin{equation}
-  \mathbf{s}=\left(\frac{2}{3},\frac{1}{3}\right),
-  \qquad
-  hs_x+ks_y=\frac{2h+k}{3},
-  \label{eq:pbi2_slip_vector}
-\end{equation}
-so the default PbI$_2$ slip phase is
-\begin{equation}
-  \delta(h,k)=2\pi\left(\frac{2h+k}{3}\right).
-  \label{eq:pbi2_delta}
-\end{equation}
-If $\tau_j\in\{0,1\}$ marks whether interface $j$ slips, the cumulative basal offset of layer $n$ is
-\begin{equation}
-  \mathbf{u}_n=\left(\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}\tau_j\right)\mathbf{s}\pmod{1},
-  \qquad n=0,\ldots,N-1.
-  \label{eq:pbi2_cumulative_shift}
-\end{equation}
-Thus a no-slip interface leaves the next trilayer in the same lateral registry, while a slip interface advances the next trilayer by $\mathbf{s}$. The all-slip three-layer sequence gives offsets $(0,0)$, $(2/3,1/3)$, and $(1/3,2/3)$, then returns to $(0,0)$ on the fourth layer. This is the ABCA stacking shown in Figure.\ref{fig:pbi2_ewald_cylinder_comparison}. 
-
-The active convention first remaps the reported parameter through
-\begin{equation}
-  p_{\mathrm{flip}}=1-p.
-  \label{eq:pbi2_pflip}
-\end{equation}
-With the slip phase in Eq.~\eqref{eq:pbi2_delta}, the layer-step average is
-\begin{equation}
-  z=(1-p_{\mathrm{flip}})+p_{\mathrm{flip}}e^{i\delta},
-  \qquad
-  f=|z|,
-  \qquad
-  \psi=\arg z.
-  \label{eq:pbi2_zfpsi}
-\end{equation}
-The out-of-plane phase sampled by the rod model is
-\begin{equation}
-  \phi(h,k,L)=\delta(h,k)+\frac{2\pi L}{\phi_{L,\mathrm{div}}},
-  \label{eq:pbi2_phi}
-\end{equation}
-where $\phi_{L,\mathrm{div}}$ is the divisor defining the active $L$ phase convention. 
-For thin films with finite-layers,
-\begin{equation}
-  R_N(h,k,L)=
-  1+\frac{2}{N}\operatorname{Re}\!\sum_{n=1}^{N-1}(N-n)t^n,
-  \qquad
-  t=f\,e^{i[\phi(h,k,L)-\psi]}.
-  \label{eq:pbi2_rn}
-\end{equation}
-In the ordered limit, the this collapses back toward the discrete Bragg sequence. As the effective layer-to-layer correlation decreases, coherent peak weight is redistributed into broader between-peak intensity along the same rod.
-
-When several local stacking environments are allowed, the calculation constructs multiple disordered curves and combines them with raw weights $w_\mu$. After normalization,
-\begin{equation}
-  \tilde{w}_\mu=\frac{w_\mu}{\sum_\nu w_\nu},
-  \qquad
-  I^{\mathrm{mix}}_{hk}(L)=\sum_{\mu=1}^{M}\tilde{w}_\mu I^{(\mu)}_{hk}(L).
-  \label{eq:pbi2_mixture}
-\end{equation}
-The weights are therefore interpreted as effective fractions within the selected polytype family. 
-\subsection{Refinement}
-
-
-The selected-rod profile is fit directly. The known Bragg positions for the convolution of polytypes in a Bragg rod define the centers of the profile terms inherited from the fixed detector geometry and ordered structural model. The disorder parameters control the redistribution of intensity among those terms and the diffuse between-peak signal through Eqs.~\eqref{eq:pbi2_translation_phase}--\eqref{eq:pbi2_rn}.
-
-Beam and detector terms, sample alignment, mosaicity, profile broadening, lattice constants, and the ordered PbI$_2$ rod intensities are fixed first by the prior method for ordered structures. The disorder model is then asked to explain the remaining systematic diffuse signal on selected m/L rods. This ordering is important because a diffuse feature should be interpreted as stacking information only after the detector geometry and orientation distribution have already been constrained by the ordered Bragg features.
-
-The residual minimized in this stage has the form
-\begin{equation}
-  r_j=\frac{I_j^{\mathrm{meas}}-I_j^{\mathrm{model}}(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\mathrm{dis}})}{\sigma_j},
-  \label{eq:pbi2_disorder_profile_residual}
-\end{equation}
-where $\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\mathrm{dis}}$ contains the slip probability, finite-stack length, declared mixture weights, and any explicitly allowed nuisance scale or background terms. \(j\) indexes sampled points along the selected rod profile, and
-\(\sigma_j\) is the assigned intensity uncertainty for point \(j\).
-The fit is accepted only if it improves the selected rod profile without destroying the ordered detector-space agreement established by the upstream refinement stages.
